Z||000004||MODIFIE||04-11-2001||08:47 PM||||I called two parts stores to check your replies autozone & motorparts both told me that it calls for roller.  so does any one know if the crank can be changed with a better one out of a other 4 cyl engine<BR> <BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B>As far as I know, '87-'88 were only years with roller.<P>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 04-11-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-11-2001||09:52 PM||||The 1988 2.5L engine has a balancer in the middle of the crankshaft that adds some strength and reduces the harmonics.<P>I have read many posts, and seen quite a few websites too, that say the 1988 Iron Duke crankshaft will fit in engines from 1984 to 1987.  <P>Here's a site that will tell you all about the 2.5L Iron Duke . . .<BR> <A HREF="http://fiero2m4.homestead.com/IronDuke.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero2m4.homestead.com/IronDuke.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||04-12-2001||04:36 PM||||GM Performance used to make a crankshaft for the performance version of the Iron Duke called the Super Duty. You may be able to use it in a production version but I'm not sure. Do a search on the Super Duty Duke and see what you find. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||MrPBody||04-12-2001||07:37 PM||||<I>re:</I> tappets:<P>I have recently looked inside two '85 4-cylinders; both had roller tappets.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||MODIFIE||04-12-2001||08:05 PM||||thanck you all that posted this ???? I now know for a fact that 86 @ up had roller lifts.  I all so have a other ? aboult the crank i have read the bottom end is good for 150+- HP. Maybe 180 with the right components (88 crank/S10 block).http://www.homestead.com/fiero2m4/DukeFAQ.html but will this block work in the 86 coupe fiero i have read the starter is on the other side and what does (DIS) mean when talking aboult the distributor it has the block in mine has one don't they all? <P>some off you my be thanking way a iron duke because thats what i have and i do not want to go though the head ack of wiring @ modifing the supension for ext weight and playing with the PROM to get it right so what i am doing is trying making the best out of what i have <BR>||||reg||12
Z||000009||88formula||04-12-2001||09:35 PM||||If you want to make the best of what you have than get an Iron Duke from a 91 Grand Am. It is rated @ 110 horsepower and has the balance shafts. I had one of those engines and it had decent power for a four cylinder especially after I rebuilt it. The redline was 5500 rpm too not 4500 rpm. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||862M4inCA||04-13-2001||06:18 AM||||hrm going from a standard distributor ignition system to a DIS (distributor-less ignition system) is a pretty nasty job from what I hear which requires swapping out the ECM as well as the engine wiring harness. Probably no worse than doing a standard engine swap, but I think you'd be looking at more trouble that it's worth especially since you mentioned not wanting to mess with PROM and ECM issues. My suggestion to you would be to either try for the stronger '88 crank, fork out the cash for the SD crank (if applicable) or just do a standard rebuild on the current duke. I'd also suggest trying to go to the roller lifter cam setup as well if you could. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||ChadMan||04-13-2001||09:26 AM||||OK, 88Formula, I have been wondering this for a long time.  Do you know where the extra 12 hp comes from in the '91 Duke (versus the '87-'88)?  Was it a redesigned intake and/or exhaust manifold?  Or was the cylinder head redesigned?  New cam?<P>I've wondered if the '88 Duke could be rebuilt to '91 specs, or if you need the whole '91 engine.  (Hope you see this semi-off-topic post...sorry Modifie)||||reg||3
